/*
// elkex:category
Labels > Node

// elkex:label
Node Label and Parent Size

// elkex:doc
The example illustrates how one can configure parent nodes to consider the size of their labels.
The layout option `nodeLabels.placement` is a combination of three components:
the vertical alignment, the horizontal alignment, and whether the label should be placed inside (the only option considered in this example) or outside of the node.
Not every combination makes sense though.
A label placed on the outside cannot be centered in both dimensions, for instance.

The option can either be set for a node or for each label individually.
Here, I, however, only set it on the node.

However, only setting the node label placement does not automatically reserve space for the label.
The option `nodeSize.constraints: NODEL_LABELS` has to be used for that.

Here, I show how the different placements affects the size of the parent node.
Centering a label inside a node does not change the size of the node.
However, if a label is placed at the top or bottom of a node, the node has to reserve space for it.
The same is true for labels placed at the top left or top right of a node.
The option `nodeSize.options: ASYMMETRICAL` can be used to disable
the automatic adjustment of the node size.
However, this option only works for nodes without children and not for parent nodes.
Additionally, whether the parent node reserves space for the label depends on the layout algorithm used: Only ELK layered supports this option fully, while other algorithms may not support it at all or only partially.

The layout algorithm computes a position for the top-left corner of the label,
relative to the top-left corner of the parent node.
This has to be considered whenever labels are positioned with a different reference point,
which is the default behavior of SVGs, for instance.
Additionally note that the layout algorithm is not able to compute the dimensions of a label,
as it isn't aware of the used font size and font family.
*/

// elkex:graph
elk.algorithm: rectpacking // Only for presentation
// Please note that the text size estimation in elklive is not accurate and that the label sizes set here are only estimates
node childWithLabelCenter {
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_CENTER V_CENTER"
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS"
	label "childWithLabelCenter" {
		layout [size: 145, 12]
	}
	label "childWithLabelCenter2" {
		layout [size: 150, 12]
	}
}

node childWithLabelTopLeft {
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_LEFT V_TOP" // This reverse space on the opposite site
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS"
	label "childWithLabelTopLeft" {
		layout [size: 145, 12]
	}
	label "childWithLabelTopLeft2" {
		layout [size: 150, 12]
	}
}

node childWithLabelTopLeftAsymmetric {
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_LEFT V_TOP" // This reverse space on the opposite site ...
	nodeSize.options: ASYMMETRICAL				// .. which is disabled here.
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS"
	label "childWithLabelTopLeftAsymmetric" {
		layout [size: 230, 12]
	}
	label "childWithLabelTopLeftAsymmetric2" {
		layout [size: 235, 12]
	}
}

node parentWithLabelCenter {
	elk.algorithm: layered // The default algorithm supports these options, while other algorithm do not
	// The label does not respect where the child nodes are placed since the labels should be centered
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_CENTER V_CENTER"
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS MINIMUM_SIZE"
	label "parentWithLabelCenter" {
		layout [size: 160, 12]
	}
	label "parentWithLabelCenter2" {
		layout [size: 160, 12]
	}

	node n1

	node n2
}

node parentWithLabelSpaceTop {
	// elk.algorithm: rectpacking // Not every algorithm supports these options fully as it is the case for the default ELK layered algorithm
	// The node reserves space at the top for the top label
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_CENTER V_TOP"
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS MINIMUM_SIZE"
	label "parentWithLabelTop" {
		layout [size: 135, 12]
	}
	label "parentWithLabelTop2" {
		layout [size: 140, 12]
	}

	node n1

	node n2
}

node parentWithLabelSpaceBottom {
	// The node reserves space at the bottom for the bottom label
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_CENTER V_BOTTOM"
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS MINIMUM_SIZE"
	label "parentWithLabelBottom" {
		layout [size: 160, 12]
	}
	label "parentWithLabelBottom2" {
		layout [size: 165, 12]
	}

	node n1

	node n2
}

node parentWithLabelSpaceTopLeft {
	// The node reserves space at the bottom for the bottom label
	nodeLabels.placement: "INSIDE H_LEFT V_TOP"
	nodeSize.constraints: "NODE_LABELS MINIMUM_SIZE"
	nodeSize.options: ASYMMETRICAL // This option only works for nodes with children
	label "parentWithLabelSpaceTopLeft" {
		layout [size: 200, 12]
	}
	label "parentWithLabelSpaceTopLeft2" {
		layout [size: 205, 12]
	}

	node n1

	node n2
}